**What**
The README leads with a safety argument built on exit code 3 -- truncation at a cap, which a gate must treat as failure. There is no cap and no exit 3 anywhere in the code. `DEVPLAN.md` already knows this and says so; the two user-facing documents do not.

**Where**
- `README.md:23` -- "Exit codes follow grep: 0 rows, 1 none, 2 error -- plus 3 for truncation at a cap"
- `docs/query-and-rules.adoc:463-465` (normative) -- "Truncation at any cap is announced by a distinct exit status. Exit codes: 0 rows, 1 no rows, 2 error, 3 truncation."
- `crates/git-query/src/main.rs:95-104` -- `main` returns only 0, 1, 2. No third arm.
- `DEVPLAN.md:803` -- "Truncation is not expressible... Exit code 3 therefore has no producer."
- `DEVPLAN.md:1050` -- "Do not fake exit 3... Keep the code reserved and documented as reserved."

The replacement the DEVPLAN specifies -- an admission-time refusal that counts the EDB, projects the cost, and declines to start -- does not exist either. `crates/gix-query-eval/src/demand.rs:112-121` enumerates every all-free EDB predicate in `rewritten.eager` in full, into an in-memory CSV string, with no count, no projection, and no ceiling.

**Why it matters**
This is the single most quotable paragraph in the README and the one a security-minded audience member will remember. It is also the one most likely to be checked: `git query run ... ; echo $?` is a two-second live disproof. Worse, someone who reads the normative spec and writes a gate around `[ $? -eq 3 ]` has written a branch that never fires, which is exactly the false confidence the paragraph exists to prevent.

The unbounded eager load is the same hole from the other side. The README argues that mode discipline converts an unaffordable query into a compile error, but an EDB predicate that legitimately offers an all-free vector is materialized whole with no ceiling, so the affordability argument holds for rules and not for the base relations they read.

**Options**
- Option A -- implement the admission-time refusal the DEVPLAN specifies: before round 1, count each eager relation, refuse above a configurable ceiling with exit 2 and a message naming the relation and the count. Cheap, honest, matches the plan. Costs a ref-count pass per eager relation.
- Option B -- implement a real post-hoc cap with a genuine exit 3, propagating a truncation flag up through derived relations. Faithful to the README but requires a truncation bit in `LoopResult` that survives every join, and Nemo cannot supply it, so every derived relation downstream of a truncated one has to be conservatively marked. Substantial work.
- Option C -- correct the README and the spec to describe the refusal, reserve 3, and put the reasoning on the slide as a design argument rather than a shipped feature.

**Recommendation**
A plus C, before the talk. The reasoning about why a truncated relation has no safe reading is genuinely good and deserves the slide; make the slide say "so we refuse to start" rather than "so we exit 3". Then ship the admission check so the refusal is real. High confidence -- this is a claim the code contradicts, not a judgement call.